//! Deterministic, headless rendering of Scrozz UI surfaces.
//!
//! This is the mechanism decision **D25** exists to create: *every* Scrozz
//! screenshot is generated by this module, and none is ever taken by hand —
//! which would be a delicious failure mode for a screenshot app.
//!
//! # Why this module is load-bearing
//!
//! An agent cannot see. The UI spike painted an unrounded scrim over a rounded
//! thumbnail — squaring the bottom corners, exactly the defect class D9 exists
//! to prevent — and never noticed, while the maintainer spotted it in seconds.
//! Golden images convert *"a human has to look at it"* into *"the build fails."*
//! Without that, every visual regression needs a human; with it, agents can work
//! the UI unattended and D5 holds.
//!
//! # One harness, three consumers
//!
//! | Consumer | [`Profile`] | What differs |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | Golden-image tests | [`Profile::Golden`] | Fixed scale, committed baselines, CI fails on any pixel diff |
//! | Store assets | [`Profile::Store`] | Exact per-store pixel dimensions, 2×/3×, localised |
//! | README and docs | [`Profile::Docs`] | Annotated stills, plus frame sequences for motion |
//!
//! The three share every line of rendering code. A store asset is therefore of
//! the **real** UI rather than a mockup, and it cannot go stale, because it
//! regenerates from the code that ships.
//!
//! # Determinism, which is the whole game
//!
//! A flaky golden test gets disabled within a week and then the entire apparatus
//! is worthless. So determinism is designed in, not hoped for:
//!
//! * **The clock is virtual.** [`VirtualClock`] is *frozen* for the whole render
//!   — every warm-up pass sees the identical timestamp — so a render names an
//!   instant ("card entry at 180 ms") rather than catching whichever frame the
//!   scheduler happened to produce. There is no wall clock anywhere in this file.
//! * **The RNG is seeded and reproducible.** [`Rng`] is a fixed-seed SplitMix64
//!   with no OS entropy and no global state.
//! * **State is a fixture, not the world.** [`Fixture`]s carry frozen
//!   timestamps derived from [`FROZEN_EPOCH_UNIX`], never `SystemTime::now()`,
//!   and synthetic thumbnails generated from the seed, never files on disk.
//! * **Nothing live sits behind glass.** [`Background::Studio`] paints a seeded
//!   synthetic backdrop so translucent surfaces have something to composite
//!   over without any real desktop content.
//! * **The rasteriser is on the CPU.** [`SoftwareRenderer`] tessellates through
//!   `epaint` and rasterises with plain IEEE-754 arithmetic, so the output is
//!   bit-identical on macOS, Windows and Linux CI. A GPU path would make the
//!   baseline depend on the driver, which is a flake generator pointed straight
//!   at the one thing that must never flake.
//! * **Fonts are pinned** by the exact `egui`/`epaint` version pin (D22). A
//!   [`Scene`] that installs custom fonts must embed them with `include_bytes!`;
//!   loading a font by family name from the host is a determinism bug.
//!
//! # The virtual clock is also the animation exporter
//!
//! Stepping the clock and dumping the frames is how animated store previews and
//! README captures are produced — see [`SoftwareRenderer::render_sequence`].
//! One mechanism, two payoffs.
//!
//! # Wiring a real surface in
//!
//! Surfaces are not hard-coded here. A surface implements [`Scene`] in its own
//! module and is registered into a [`SceneRegistry`]; until it is, the harness
//! renders a clearly watermarked [`PlaceholderScene`], and
//! [`Profile::Store`] **refuses** to render a placeholder so a stand-in can
//! never reach a store listing.

